                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal construction
                      %ul
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"}> limit
                            \/
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weighted+limit"} weighted limit
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"}> end
                            \/
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
                  %h2#sidebar_theorems Theorems
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} Isbell duality
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor+theorem"} adjoint functor theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadicity+theorem"} monadicity theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+lifting+theorem"} adjoint lifting theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel-Ulmer duality
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd-Mitchell+embedding+theorem"} Freyd-Mitchell embedding theorem
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
                  %h2#sidebar_extensions Extensions
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+and+topos+theory"} sheaf and topos theory
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
                    %li
                      %p
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
                  %h2#sidebar_applications Applications
                  %ul
                    %li
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/applications+of+%28higher%29+category+theory"} applications of (higher) category theory
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/category+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %p
            There are a number of different types of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            bearing the name
            = succeed "," do
              %strong fibration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag"} zigzag
            of relationships. For the fibrations in category theory, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#classical_homotopy_theory"} Classical homotopy theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#abstract_homotopy_theory"} Abstract homotopy theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#transports_and_classifying_spaces"} Transports and classifying spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#fibrations_in_category_theory"} Fibrations in category theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#fibrations_in_higher_category_theory"} Fibrations in higher category theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#fibrations_in_type_theory"} Fibrations in type theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
          %h2#classical_homotopy_theory Classical homotopy theory
          %p
            In classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:E\to B
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            that has a certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+property"} lifting property
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in E
            and a path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1] \to B
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            starting at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p(e)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            starting at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
          %p
            One generally also assumes the lifting of additional structures (including “higher homotopies”) in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            which, in particular, imply that the path lifting is unique up to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+fibration"} Hurewicz fibration
            %li
              %p
                In a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibration"} Serre fibration
                = succeed "-cells" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                In a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold+fibration"} Dold fibration
                or “halb-fibration,” all homotopies can be lifted, but the lifting only has to agree with the given initial map up to vertical homotopy. A Hurewicz fibration is a Dold fibration where the vertical homotopy is stationary.
          %p
            All three of these definitions give rise to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequence+of+homotopy+groups"} long exact sequence of homotopy groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
            would follow from only requiring up-to-homotopy lifting for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubes"} cubes
          %ul
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasifibration"} quasifibration
              (not to be confused with the completely different notion related to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
              below) is a map which induces a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequence+of+homotopy+groups"} long exact sequence of homotopy groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibers"} fibers
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weakly homotopy equivalent
              to the corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber"} homotopy fiber
          %h2#abstract_homotopy_theory Abstract homotopy theory
          %p
            Inspired by the role of fibrations in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            is a class of maps called “fibrations,” which also possess a lifting property relating them to the rest of the structure (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrations"} Kan fibrations
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                (whose study predates the definition of a
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplices"} simplices
                for every
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibration"}> isofibration
                s between
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            %li
              %p
                fibrations in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+quasi-categories"} model structure for quasi-categories
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
                are a common generalization of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrations"} Kan fibrations
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibrations"} isofibrations
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasicategories
                are a common generalization of
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
          %p
            Fibrations have many good properties in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            of a fibration is already a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            (see there for details). Sufficient conditions are that in addition all three
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            involved are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
            or that the model category is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+proper+model+category"} right proper model category
          %p
            Generally, every morphism can be replaced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weakly equivalent
            fibration, which gives one way to compute a homotopy pullback by comuting an ordinary pullback of a fibrant enough weakly quivalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cospan"} cospan
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
          %h2#transports_and_classifying_spaces Transports and classifying spaces
          %p
            There is a classical theorem that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+spaces"} covering spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:E\to B
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+space"} locally connected space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            (which have
            %em unique
            path lifting) are equivalent to functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(B)\to Set
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            (hence to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+representations"} permutation representations
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            ).
          %p
            The functor corresponding to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:E\to B
            takes a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
            to its fiber
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{-1}(b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b'
            to the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{-1}(b) \to p^{-1}(b')
            defined by “the endpoint of the lift of
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p
            Generalizing this massively, arbitrary topological fibrations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:E\to B
            correspond to functors from the fundamental
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            corresponding to the fibration. There are a number of ways to make this precise, some of which make sense in classical homotopy theory (e.g. actions by a loop space) and some of which require higher categorical machinery. A discussion is at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport"} higher parallel transport
            in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/higher+parallel+transport#FlatInTop"} Flat ∞-parallel transport in Top
          %p
            More generally, one can consider fibrations in which the fibers are equipped with some extra structure, such as being a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            or having an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain"} codomain
            of the transport to some category of spaces with structure and structure-preserving maps. The most common version of this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
            with some structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with a single object (thought of as a generic
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as its endomorphisms. In such cases the word “parallel” is often added in front of “transport.”
          %p
            If we replace the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groupoid"} topological groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}G
            – which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+simplicial+topological+spaces"} geometric realization of simplicial topological spaces
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            – then passing to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0
            recovers the classical fact that “classifying spaces classify”: there is a bijection between
            = succeed "-bundles" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and homotopy classes of maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to \mathcal{B}G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            the “universal
            = succeed "-bundle\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}G \to \mathcal{B}G
            over the space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B}G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
          %h2#fibrations_in_category_theory Fibrations in category theory
          %p
            It is common in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to consider the objects of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over+category"} slice category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C/X
            as “objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            varying over
            = succeed ".\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to X
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/X
            can be identified with an
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{A_x\}_{x\in X}
            of sets, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_x
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to X
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top/X
            as a family of spaces (the fibers) “varying continuously” over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Top
            we need the map to be a fibration.
          %p
            In category theory, there is analogous notion of when a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:E\to B
            is a
            %strong fibration
            or
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibered category
            or
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong Cartesian fibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \mapsto p^{-1}(b)
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-functor"} pseudo-functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\to Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+functor"} covariant
            or
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functor"} contravariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is the analogue of the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"}> classifying space
            ”, and there is a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+Cartesian+fibration"}> universal Cartesian fibration
            ”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_* \to Cat
            of which every other fibration is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            (modulo size issues).
          %p
            Categorical fibrations also have a “lifting” property, but the liftings must satisfy an extra “universality” condition. For this reason, they are not the fibrations in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure"} model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            are the same as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isofibrations"} isofibrations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/folk+model+structure"} folk model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
            for more details.
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+fibration"} discrete fibration
            is one in which we use
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            instead of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            as the classifying space.
          %h2#fibrations_in_higher_category_theory Fibrations in higher category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations+of+quasi-categories"} fibrations of quasi-categories
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+fibration"} Cartesian fibration
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+fibration"}> left fibration
                    ,
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+fibration"} right fibration
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right%2Fleft+fibration+of+simplicial+spaces"} right/left fibration of simplicial spaces
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+Kan+fibration"} inner Kan fibration
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+Joyal+fibration"} minimal Joyal fibration
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+fibration"} homotopy Kan fibration
          %h2#fibrations_in_type_theory Fibrations in type theory
          %p
            Fibrations are employed in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+models+of+dependent+types"} categorical models of dependent types
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %p
            Both forms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+2-category"} 2-categorical dualization
            are commonly encountered in the context of fibrations. Moreover, the distinction between the two is appreciable.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cofibrations"} Cofibrations
            play a role dual to that of fibrations in homotopy theory, notably in the axioms for a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opfibrations"} opfibrations
            are the same as fibrations homotopically because of the invertibility of 2-cells. The duality between fibrations and opfibrations is more visible in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            such that the unique morphism to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            is a fibration (in abstract
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+object"} fibrant object
          %p
            A replacement of a morphism by a weakly equivalent fibration is also called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
